current backend setup:

Spring STOMP over WebSocket

Broker destinations: /topic/**

STOMP endpoint: /ws

REST API: /api/runs/**

Realtime payload pushed by backend: RunView on /topic/runs/{runId}

1) Connectivity Contract
1.1 Transport

Realtime channel: STOMP over WebSocket

WebSocket endpoint (handshake): GET ws(s)://<core-host>/ws

Broker destinations: /topic/** (server-to-client fanout)

App destinations: /app/** (client-to-server commands, optional in v1)

1.2 Authentication & Session

You must choose one (the frontend implementation differs):

Cookie-based session (recommended for browser simplicity)

WebSocket handshake relies on browser cookies (SameSite=None; Secure if cross-site).

REST calls: credentials: "include".


2) API Surface the UI uses
2.1 Create a run

POST /api/runs

Creates a ProcessRun

Required fields (minimum):

processType (enum string, e.g. LEAK_DETECTION)

edgeId (string; optional if your flow doesn’t need subsystem lookup)

contextJson (stringified JSON; default {})

Response

ProcessRun object; UI must capture run.id

2.2 Fetch current projection (recommended)

GET /api/runs/{runId}/view

Returns RunView, the canonical UI projection.

Used for:

initial render

recovery after reconnect

idempotent refresh

2.3 Apply trigger (start/stop/…)

POST /api/runs/{runId}/trigger/{trigger}

Returns updated RunView

UI should treat REST response as immediate/authoritative, but still accept the subsequent WS push.

2.4 Commands API (optional for v1)

POST /api/runs/{runId}/commands/{command}

Requires header Idempotency-Key

This is more “distributed-systems correct” for edge orchestration, but you can start with triggers.

3) Realtime Channel Contract
3.1 Subscription

After runId is known, the frontend opens a WebSocket and establishes a STOMP session, then subscribes to:

Topic: /topic/runs/{runId}

3.2 Message payload

Backend sends:

RunView JSON (whatever AllowedCommandsService.buildRunView(...) produces)

The UI must treat incoming RunView as the single source of truth for:

run state (state)

allowed actions (e.g. allowed triggers / commands list)

subsystem statuses (if included)

guard/validation results (if included)

3.3 Delivery semantics

Broker is enableSimpleBroker → best-effort delivery.

Frontend must implement resilience patterns:

automatic reconnect (client-side)

on reconnect, call GET /view to resync (because messages may be missed while offline)

local “optimistic UI” should be replaced by incoming RunView (server-authoritative)

4) Frontend State Model
4.1 Core state

Maintain a minimal client-side state machine:

runId: string | null

realtimeStatus: "disconnected" | "connecting" | "connected" | "error"

runView: RunView | null

lastError?: string

lastUpdateTs?: number

4.2 Deterministic render strategy

Render UI strictly from runView (projection-driven UI).

Buttons enabled/disabled based on:

runView.allowedTriggers (or equivalent)

runView.allowedCommands (or equivalent)

Avoid deriving state from local flags (prevents UI drift).

5) Frontend Implementation Requirements
5.1 Libraries

STOMP client: @stomp/stompjs

(Optional) SockJS: only if you enable .withSockJS() server-side

5.2 WebSocket URL resolution

If frontend is served behind the same origin/proxy as core:

ws(s)://window.location.host/ws

If different origin:

ws(s)://<core-host>/ws from NEXT_PUBLIC_CORE_WS_URL

5.3 Reconnect policy

reconnect backoff: constant or exponential (e.g. start 1s, cap at 10s)

on reconnect:

re-subscribe to /topic/runs/{runId}

fetch GET /api/runs/{runId}/view once to resync

5.4 Error handling

If REST trigger returns:

409 (optimistic lock): UI retries a GET /view

422 (guard violation): UI displays guard reason + snapshot

400/404: show error toast and stop

5.5 CORS / credentials

REST: credentials: "include" if cookie auth

WS: cookies automatically included by browser in handshake if same-site constraints allow.

If Bearer auth: connectHeaders must include token, and backend must accept it for WS.

6) UI Interaction “Start Process”
6.1 Control sequence (recommended)

POST /api/runs → obtain runId

open WS + STOMP connect → subscribe /topic/runs/{runId}

GET /api/runs/{runId}/view → initial snapshot

user clicks Start

POST /api/runs/{runId}/trigger/START

render returned RunView

subsequent WS messages keep UI consistent

6.2 Idempotency & replay-safe behavior

If the user double-clicks Start:

backend may ignore replay-safe triggers (as your engine does)

UI should disable Start when not allowed by runView

7) Minimal Acceptance Criteria

The frontend integration is “done” when:

From UI you can create a run and see its RunView.

Clicking “Start” transitions run state and updates UI immediately (REST response).

UI receives at least one realtime update via /topic/runs/{runId} on state changes.

Refreshing the page and reloading with runId restores state via GET /view.

Losing network temporarily reconnects and rehydrates state via GET /view.